WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.160
Let's say you're trying to lose 20 pounds,
or boost your immunity,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.680
or increase your ability to fight COVID,
or even cancer.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Well, the amazing thing is,
with the right diet,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.040
you're well on your way to
achieving these vital health goals.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.480
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts Podcast.
I'm your host, Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.680
Today we look at
how losing weight

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.960
without rearranging your
gastrointestinal anatomy

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.880
carries advantages beyond
just the lack of surgical risk.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.800
The surgical community objects
to the characterization

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.400
of bariatric surgery as
merely internal jaw wiring,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.840
cutting into healthy organs just
to discipline people’s behavior.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.880
They’ve gone as far as to
rename it “metabolic surgery,”

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.240
suggesting the anatomical
rearrangements cause changes

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.240
in digestive hormones that
offer unique physiological benefits.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.560
As evidence, they
point to the remarkable

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.880
remission rates for type 2 diabetes.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.360
After bariatric surgery,
about 50% of obese diabetics

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.240
and 75% of super-obese
diabetics go into remission,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.760
meaning they have
normal blood sugars

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.880
on a regular diet off
all diabetes medications.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.680
The normalization in
blood sugars can happen

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.240
within literally days after the surgery.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.800
And then fifteen years after
the surgery, 30% may remain free

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.840
from their diabetes
(compared to a 7% cure rate

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.320
in a nonsurgical control group).

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.120
Are we sure it was the surgery, though?

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.040
One of the most challenging
parts of bariatric surgery

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.880
is lifting the liver.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.920
Since obese individuals tend
to have such large fatty livers,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.520
this can be tricky, risking
liver injury and bleeding.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.760
Enlarged livers are one of the
most common reasons less invasive

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.280
laparoscopic surgery turns into
to fully invasive open surgery,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.240
leaving the patient with a large
full-belly scar, and increased

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.120
risk of wound infections,
complications, and recovery time.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.720
But lose even just 5%
of your body weight

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.880
and your fatty liver may shrink by 10%.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.280
That’s why those awaiting bariatric
surgery are put on a diet.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.320
Then after surgery,
patients are typically placed

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.200
on an extremely low-calorie
liquid diet for weeks.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.240
Could their improvement
in blood sugars just be

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.040
from the calorie restriction,
rather than some sort of

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.200
surgical metabolic magic?

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.880
Researchers decided to put it to the test.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.560
At a bariatric surgery clinic
at the University of Texas,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.080
patients with type 2 diabetes
scheduled for gastric bypass

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.800
volunteered to first
undergo an identical period

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.240
of calorie restriction
but without the surgery.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.960
They were placed in the hospital
and put on the same diet

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.640
they would be on immediately before
and after the surgery for ten days,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.360
averaging less than 500 calories a
day to mimic the surgical situation.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.760
Then, the researchers waited
a few months so the patients

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.640
would gain the weight back and,
then put them through the actual

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.080
surgery matched day-to-day
to the diets they were on before.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.480
So, the same patients, same diets,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.960
just with or without the actual surgery.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.520
If there was some sort
of metabolic benefit

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.160
to the anatomical rearrangement,
they would have done better

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.640
after the actual surgery, but in
some ways they actually did worse.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.680
The calorie restriction alone
resulted in similar improvements

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.120
in blood sugar, pancreatic
function, and insulin sensitivity,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.840
but several measures of
diabetic control improved

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.080
significantly more without the surgery.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.680
So, the surgery seemed to put
them at a metabolic disadvantage.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.920
The calorie restriction works by
first mobilizing fat out of the liver.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.560
Type 2 diabetes is thought
to be caused by fat building up

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.680
in the liver and then spilling
over into the pancreas.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.240
Everyone may have a
“personal fat threshold”

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.240
for the safe storage of excess fat.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.360
When that limit is exceeded,
fat gets deposited in the liver,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.080
where it causes insulin resistance.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.200
The liver attempts to offload some
of the fat (in the form of a fat

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.920
transport molecule called VLDL),
which then gets stuck in the pancreas

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.320
and can kill off the
cells that produce insulin.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.360
By the time diabetes
is diagnosed, half of our

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.680
insulin-producing cells
may have been destroyed.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.480
Put people on a low-calorie diet, though,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.360
and this entire process can be reversed.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.760
A large enough negative
calorie balance can cause

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.160
a profound drop in liver
fat, sufficient to resurrect

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.960
liver insulin sensitivity
within seven days.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.720
Keep it up, and the liver stops
spitting out fat enough to help

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.560
normalize pancreatic fat levels
and function within just eight weeks.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.240
Once you drop below your
personal fat threshold,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.480
you should then be able
to resume normal intake

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.560
and still keep your diabetes at bay.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.400
The bottom line is that
type 2 diabetes is reversible

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.360
with weight loss, if you
catch it early enough.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.040
Lose more than 30 pounds and
nearly 90% of those who have had

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.240
type 2 diabetes for less than
four years can achieve remission,

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.280
whereas it may only be reversible
in 50% of whose who’ve lived

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.440
with the disease for
longer than eight years.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.960
That’s losing weight
with diet alone, though.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.520
The remission numbers for
diabetics losing even more

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.480
than twice as much weight
with bariatric surgery

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.120
may only be around 75% up
to 8 years and 40% after that.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.960
Losing weight without
resorting to surgery

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.120
may offer other benefits as well.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.400
Diabetics losing weight with diet
alone can significantly improve

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.160
markers of systemic inflammation,
such as tumor necrosis factor,

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.480
whereas levels significantly
worsened when about

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.160
the same amount of weight was
lost from a gastric bypass.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.800
What about diabetic complications?

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.280
One of the reasons we don’t want
diabetes is that we don’t want

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.840
to go blind, and we don’t
want to have to go on dialysis.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.520
Reversing diabetes with
bariatric surgery can improve

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.360
kidney function, but
surprisingly, it may not prevent

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.720
the appearance or progression
of diabetic vision loss.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.880
Perhaps because bariatric
surgery affects diet quantity

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.520
but not necessarily diet quality.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.080
This reminds me of a famous
study published in the

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.400
New England Journal of Medicine
that randomized thousands

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.720
of diabetics to an
intensive lifestyle program

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.960
that focused on weight loss.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.920
Ten years in, the study was
stopped prematurely because

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.400
the diabetics weren’t living any longer

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.960
or having any fewer heart attacks.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.800
This may be because
they remained on the same

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.497
heart-clogging diet but
just in smaller portions.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.960
In our next story, we look
at how weight regain

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.120
after bariatric surgery can have
devastating psychological effects.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.000
How sustainable is the weight
loss from bariatric surgery?

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.720
Most gastric bypass patients end
up regaining some of the

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.760
fat they lose over the first
year or two after surgery,

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.360
but five years later three-quarters
maintain at least a 20% weight loss.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.160
The typical trajectory for someone
who starts out obese at 285 pounds,

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.920
for example, would be to drop
to an overweight 178 pounds

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.960
two years after bariatric
surgery but then regain

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.520
back up to an obese 207 pounds.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.480
This has been chalked up
to “grazing” behavior,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.560
where compulsive eaters
may shift from binging,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.120
which becomes more difficult
post-surgery, to eating

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.440
smaller amounts constantly
throughout the day.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.880
Eight years out, about half
of gastric bypass patients

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.680
continue to describe
episodes of disordered eating.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.960
As one pediatric obesity
specialist described,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.480
“I have seen many patients who put
chocolate bars into a blender

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.800
with some cream, just
to pass technically

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.120
installed obstacles
[like a gastric band].”

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Bariatric surgery advertising
is filled with happily-ever-after

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.200
fairytale narratives of cherry-
picked outcomes offering,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.160
as one ad analysis put it, “the full
Cinderella-romance happy ending.”

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.480
This may contribute to the finding
that patients often overestimate

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.600
the amount of weight they’ll
lose with the procedure and

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.880
underestimate the difficulty
of the recovery process.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.040
Surgery forces profound changes
in eating habits requiring slow,

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.120
thoroughly chewed, small bites.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.920
Your stomach goes from the
volume of two softballs

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.680
to down to the size of half a
tennis ball in stomach stapling,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.840
and half of a ping-pong ball in
the case of gastric bypass or banding.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.080
As you can imagine, weight
regain after surgery can have

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.840
devastating psychological
effects, as patients may feel

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.600
they failed their last resort.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.640
This may explain why
bariatric surgery patients

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.840
are at a high risk of
depression and suicide.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.800
Now severe obesity alone may
increase risk of suicidal depression,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.680
but even at the same weight,
those going through surgery

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.760
appear to be at higher risk.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.040
At the same BMI, age, and
gender, bariatric surgery

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:30.000
recipients have nearly four
times the odds of suicide

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.560
compared with counterparts
not undergoing the procedure.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.200
Most convincingly, before-and-after
mirror-image analyses

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.280
show the risk of serious self-harm

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.120
increases post-surgery
among the same individuals.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:47.360
About 1 in 50 bariatric surgery
patients end up killing themselves

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.640
or being hospitalized for
self-harm or attempted suicide.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.600
And this only includes confirmed
suicides, excluding masked attempts

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.160
such as overdoses of
“undetermined intention.”

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.600
Bariatric surgery patients
also have an elevated risk

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.960
of accidental death,
though some of this may be

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.680
due to changes in alcohol metabolism.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.640
Give gastric bypass
folks two shots of vodka,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.680
and because of their altered
anatomy their blood alcohol level

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.480
shoots past the legal driving
limit within minutes.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.560
It’s unclear whether this
plays a role in the 25% increase

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.640
in prevalence of alcohol problems noted

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.480
during the second postoperative year.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.840
Even those who successfully
lose their excess weight

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:31.360
and keep it off appear to
have a hard time coping.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.440
Ten years out, though physical health-
related quality-of-life improves,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.280
general mental health tends
to significantly deteriorate

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.200
compared to pre-surgical levels
even among the biggest losers.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.080
Ironically, there’s a common
notion that bariatric surgery

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.880
is for “cheaters” who
take the easy way out

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.320
by choosing the “low-effort”
method of weight loss.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.520
Shedding the pounds may not shed
the stigma of even prior obesity.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.960
Studies suggest that in the eyes
of others, knowing someone was fat

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.720
in the past leads them to always
be treated more like a fat person.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.920
And there’s a strong anti-
surgery bias on top of that,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.320
such that those who choose
the scalpel to lose weight

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.280
are rated most negatively
(for example, being considered

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.160
less physically attractive).
One can imagine how

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.200
remaining a target of prejudice
even after joining the

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.440
“in-group” could potentially
undercut psychological well-being.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.880
There can also be unexpected
physical consequences of massive

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.240
weight loss like large hanging
flaps of excess skin.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.080
Beyond being heavy and uncomfortable,
and interfering with movement,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.720
the skin flaps can result
in itching, irritation,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.480
dermatitis, and skin infections.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.520
Getting a panniculectomy
(removing the abdominal “apron”

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.440
of hanging skin) can be expensive
and has a complication rate

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:52.960
exceeding 50%, with dehiscence
(rupturing of the surgical wound)

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:54.800
being the most common complication.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.720
“Even if surgery proves
sustainably effective,”

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.800
wrote the founding director
of Yale University’s

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.160
Prevention Research Center, “the
need to rely on the rearrangement

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.680
of our natural gastrointestinal
anatomy as an alternative

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.400
to better use of feet and
forks [(diet and exercise)]

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:13.840
seems a societal travesty.”

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.520
In the Middle Ages, starving peasants
dreamed of gastronomic utopias

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.080
where food just rained
down from the sky.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.880
The English called it
the Kingdom of Cockaigne.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.400
Little could medieval fabulists predict
that many of their descendants

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.760
would not only take permanent
residence there, but cut out

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.960
parts of their stomachs and
intestines to combat the abundance.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:41.200
Critics have pointed out the
irony of surgically altering

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.320
healthy organs to make them&nbsp;
dysfunctional—mal-absorptive—

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:48.720
on purpose, especially when
it comes to operating on children.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.160
Bariatric surgery for kids and
teens has become widespread

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.840
and is being performed on children
as young as five years old.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.840
Surgeons defend the practice
by arguing that growing up fat

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.160
can leave emotional scars and
“lifelong social retardation.”

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.560
Promoters of preventive medicine
argue that bariatric surgery

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.000
is the proverbial “ambulance
at the bottom of the cliff.”

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.000
In response, proponents of pediatric
bariatric surgery have written:

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.480
“It is often pointed out that
we should focus on prevention.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:22.080
Of course, I agree. However,
if someone is drowning,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.480
I don’t tell them, ‘You should learn
how to swim’; no, I rescue them.”

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.080
A strong case can be made that
the benefits of bariatric surgery

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:35.920
far outweigh the risks if the alternative
is remaining morbidly obese,

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:37.840
which is estimated to
shave up to a dozen

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.600
or more years off of one’s life.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.480
Although there haven’t been any
data from randomized trials

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:46.080
to back it up yet, compared to
non-operated obese individuals,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.240
those getting bariatric
surgery would be expected

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.040
to live significantly longer on average.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.400
No wonder surgeons consistently
frame the elective surgery

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.520
as a life-or-death necessity.
This is a false dichotomy though.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.280
The benefits only outweigh the risks
if there are no other alternatives.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:06.160
Might there be a way to
lose weight healthfully

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.320
without resorting to the operating table?

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.772
That’s what my book
How Not to Diet is all about.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.360
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.400
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.920
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:28.080
We may share it on our social media
to help inspire others.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:32.080
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.520
please go to the Nutrition Facts Podcast
landing page.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.600
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.720
plus links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:47.840
For a vital, timely text on the
pathogens that cause pandemics

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.440
you can order the e-book, audio book,

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:53.200
or hard copy of my latest book
“How to Survive a Pandemic.”

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:57.600
For recipes, check out my
“How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.320
Which is my latest-latest book.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:05.360
It’s beautifully designed, with more than 100
recipes for delicious and nutritious meals.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:10.640
All the proceeds I receive from
the sales of all my books goes to charity.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:14.800
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:17.040
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.640
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.600
Everything on the website is free.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.920
There’s no ads, no corporate sponsorship.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:29.120
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.160
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.960
as a tribute to my grandmother,

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:41.360
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

